import{_ as e,o as a,c as o,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.CS-cBh1g.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"How agents work tasks","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"agents/tasks.md","filePath":"agents/tasks.md"}'),r={name:"agents/tasks.md"};function n(d,t,h,i,c,l){return a(),o("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[s('<h1 id="how-agents-work-tasks" tabindex="-1">How agents work tasks <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-agents-work-tasks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How agents work tasks&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This is the agent&#39;s side of the <a href="/Mayhem/suite/tasks">Tasks</a> board: what it reads, what it writes, and when it waits for a person. Agents connected with the prompt from <strong>Connect an agent</strong> follow it automatically. It&#39;s documented here so you know what to expect, and so you can check an agent is doing it right.</p><h2 id="where-tasks-live" tabindex="-1">Where tasks live <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-tasks-live" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where tasks live&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each task is one JSON file in the robot repo: <code>tools/mayhem/review/&lt;id&gt;.json</code>. Feedback from anywhere in the app lands there with its context, so the agent knows exactly what you meant:</p><ul><li>a <strong>log moment</strong>: the recording, seconds into it, the code revision, the field pose;</li><li>an <strong>auto step</strong>: the routine and step;</li><li>a <strong>state machine</strong>: the machine, state or transition;</li><li>anything else right-clicked: the page, panel and item.</li></ul><p>Commit task files with the code (or at least push them) so teammates and agents on other computers see them.</p><h2 id="the-loop" tabindex="-1">The loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The loop&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Agent does</th><th>Board column</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><code>list_tasks</code>, then <code>get_task</code> for the context. Opens the linked log with the log tools.</td><td>New</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><code>claim_task</code>. Mayhem records the current commit as the task&#39;s base.</td><td>Agent planning</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><code>propose_plan</code> in plain English: what will change on the robot and how you&#39;ll see it. <strong>Then waits.</strong> No code changes before approval.</td><td>Plan to approve</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>After you approve: changes the code, runs the sim proof, commits with a <code>Mayhem-Task: &lt;id&gt;</code> line, and calls <code>report_progress</code>.</td><td>Agent working</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><code>finish_task</code>, saying what it ran.</td><td>Ready to check</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If you send a plan or a change back, your note goes into the task and it returns to the agent. Your note is what it reads to try again, which is why sending back needs one.</p><h2 id="code-simulate-read-fix" tabindex="-1">Code, simulate, read, fix <a class="header-anchor" href="#code-simulate-read-fix" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Code, simulate, read, fix&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For a behavior bug, agents are expected to close the loop before asking you:</p><ol><li>Change the code, and regenerate <code>MayhemProject.h</code> with <code>tools/mayhem/export.sh</code> if the project or <code>machines.json</code> changed.</li><li>Run the relevant <a href="/Mayhem/robot-code/scenarios">sim proof scenarios</a> on the uncommitted code (<code>run_scenarios</code>).</li><li>Read the result&#39;s log: <code>log_states</code> for a state machine problem; <code>log_anomalies</code>, <code>log_what_changed</code> and <code>log_table</code> for anything else.</li><li>Fix and repeat. Before handing off, run everything against the base and add a scenario that reproduces the bug, so it stays fixed.</li></ol><h2 id="what-gets-accepted-without-you" tabindex="-1">What gets accepted without you <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-gets-accepted-without-you" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What gets accepted without you&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Mayhem rates every reported commit with the same review the <a href="/Mayhem/suite/changes">Changes</a> page shows:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Means</th><th>Accepted automatically?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Red</strong></td><td>CAN IDs, current limits, brake/coast, motor directions, gear ratios, offsets; or generated code out of date</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Yellow</strong></td><td>Driver controls, state machines, other values, any other robot code, a loosened scenario</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Green</strong></td><td>Only auto paths changed, and every changed path still finishes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>None</strong></td><td>Nothing that reaches the robot changed (notes, a new scenario)</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Most real fixes touch C++ logic that Mayhem can&#39;t read, so they&#39;re yellow and wait for a person. Judge those by the sim proof.</p><h2 id="stalled-agents" tabindex="-1">Stalled agents <a class="header-anchor" href="#stalled-agents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stalled agents&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A task an agent has held for a day without an update shows <strong>no update for …</strong>. Use <strong>Back to New</strong> to take it back; another agent (or the same one, restarted) can pick it up.</p>',18)])])}const m=e(r,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
